Wayne Marsh <wmarsh@dsl.pipex.com> writes:

> Has anybody thought about a simple source hack to make the RNG return
> the same sequence of numbers for such things as dungeon generation?

Hmh.. Basically, this means you want some random-number-functions 
which are used exclusively for, say, dungeon-generation, and 
do not use external 'randomness' like the calling of rand().  
In theory, it should be not too hard to encode that; you just 
write some new functions (rn2dg etc) into ran.c which use, say, 
a linear congruence generator with some random or nonrandom seed,   
then let all dungeon-cration stuff rely on them.  The problem with 
this is two-fold:  First, you'll have to find every 
dungeon-creation-induced random number call and replace it, which 
sounds painful but manageable; second, you'll have to pre-generate 
the whole dungeon at the start of the game (or disallow all trapdoors 
and level-porting, which you don't want to do), contradictory to the 
now-implemented-and-well-tested method and probably leading to 
a plethora of ungodly troubles.  

Maybe one could avoid fiddling with the creation routines 
by automatically creating a wiz-mode char, letting him visit the 
levels in proper order (and thus letting them be generated in the 
classical way, but with the special random-number-functions), 
then allowing (by means of a hack) the wiz character to transform 
into a 'normally generated' character in normal playing mode.  
However, messing around with the WIZMODE variable doesn't 
look too easy or trouble-save to me, too.  

So, in summary, there's no such hack, AFAIK, and I don't think 
it's all that easy to do.  

